## Critical Testing Rules

{% if variables.state_library == 'Zustand' %}
### 1. Reset Zustand Stores Before EVERY Test

Zustand stores persist state between tests by default. You MUST reset them.

```typescript
import { useFeatureStore } from '@/stores/useFeatureStore';

beforeEach(() => {
  useFeatureStore.getState().reset();
});
```
{% elif variables.state_library == 'Redux Toolkit' %}
### 1. Fresh Redux Store Per Test

Never render against the app's singleton store. Test slices as pure reducers, and
give rendered components a new store built from the same reducers in each test.

```typescript
import { configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';
import { Provider } from 'react-redux';
import { rootReducer } from '@/store'; // export the combineReducers result from src/store/index.ts

function renderWithStore(ui: React.ReactElement) {
  const store = configureStore({ reducer: rootReducer });
  return { store, ...render(<Provider store={store}>{ui}</Provider>) };
}
```
{% elif variables.state_library == 'MobX' %}
### 1. Fresh MobX Store Instance Per Test

Module-level store singletons keep state between tests. Create a new instance in
`beforeEach` (or call its `reset()`), and pass it through the provider when rendering.

```typescript
import { TransactionFilterStore } from '@/stores/TransactionFilterStore';

let store: TransactionFilterStore;
beforeEach(() => {
  store = new TransactionFilterStore();
});
```
{% elif variables.state_library == 'Jotai' %}
### 1. Fresh Jotai Store Per Test

Atoms used without a Provider share the default store across tests. Create a store
per test and render inside `<Provider store={store}>`.

```typescript
import { createStore, Provider } from 'jotai';

let store: ReturnType<typeof createStore>;
beforeEach(() => {
  store = createStore();
});
```
{% else %}
### 1. No Shared State Between Tests

There is no global store: UI state lives in components and resets with each
`render`. Don't add module-level state that would leak between tests.
{% endif %}

### 2. Fresh QueryClient Per Test

React Query caches data between tests. Always create a fresh `QueryClient`.

```typescript
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';

function createTestQueryClient() {
  return new QueryClient({
    defaultOptions: {
      queries: {
        retry: false,
        gcTime: 0,
      },
    },
  });
}

function createWrapper() {
  const queryClient = createTestQueryClient();
  return function Wrapper({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
    return (
      <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
        {children}
      </QueryClientProvider>
    );
  };
}
```

### 3. Mock Database Queries

Never hit the real database in tests. Mock Drizzle query functions.

```typescript
jest.mock('@/db/queries/features', () => ({
  getFeatures: jest.fn(),
  createFeature: jest.fn(),
}));
```

### 4. Test Screens with `renderRouter`

Don't mock expo-router for screen tests. `renderRouter` from `expo-router/testing-library`
mounts screens in an in-memory router, so links, params and guards really run.

```typescript
import { Slot } from 'expo-router';
import { renderRouter, screen } from 'expo-router/testing-library';

await renderRouter(
  {
    _layout: () => <Providers><Slot /></Providers>,  // QueryClient{% if variables.ui_library in ['Tamagui', 'Gluestack UI'] %}, {{ variables.ui_library }} provider{% endif %}{% if variables.state_library in ['Redux Toolkit', 'Jotai', 'MobX'] %}, {{ variables.state_library }} store{% endif %}

    'items/index': ItemsScreen,
    'items/[id]': ItemDetailScreen,
  },
  { initialUrl: '/items' }
);
await fireEvent.press(await screen.findByText('First item'));
expect(screen).toHavePathname('/items/1');
```

Only a component test that just calls `router.push` mocks it, in that file:
`jest.mock('expo-router', () => ({ ...jest.requireActual('expo-router'), router: { push: jest.fn() } }))`.

### 5. Await Everything (RNTL v14+)

`render`, `renderHook`, `fireEvent` and `act` return Promises in React Native Testing
Library v14 (the version `expo-router/testing-library` needs). Await them, and use
`findBy*` / `waitFor` for results that arrive later.

```typescript
await render(<TransactionCard transaction={tx} />);
await fireEvent.press(screen.getByText('Grocery shopping'));
expect(await screen.findByText('Transaction 1')).toBeOnTheScreen();
```

Assert text, roles, labels and callbacks; never style objects{% if variables.ui_library != 'StyleSheet' %} ({{ variables.ui_library }} styles don't resolve to stable style objects in Jest){% endif %}.

### 6. Mock i18next

```typescript
jest.mock('react-i18next', () => ({
  useTranslation: () => ({
    t: (key: string) => key,
    i18n: { changeLanguage: jest.fn() },
  }),
}));
```

### 7. Guard i18n Catalog Parity (CRITICAL)

This stack ships **{{ variables.locale_count }} locales** ({{ variables.locales }}).
Key drift between catalogs — a key present in `en.json` but missing (or extra) in
another locale — is the single most common i18n bug and is silent at runtime
(i18next just falls back to the key or the default locale). Every project MUST
have a test that asserts **every locale catalog has the exact same key set as the
source catalog `en.json`**. Treat `en.json` as the source of truth; all other
catalogs must match it key-for-key. See the parity test pattern below.

## Test Patterns

### Component Test

```typescript
import { fireEvent, screen } from '@testing-library/react-native';
import { renderWithProviders } from '../test-utils'; // providers + fresh QueryClient (see /test references)
import { TransactionCard } from '@/components/lists/TransactionCard';

const mockTransaction = {
  id: '1',
  description: 'Grocery shopping',
  amount: -45.99,
};

describe('TransactionCard', () => {
  it('renders transaction description and amount', async () => {
    await renderWithProviders(<TransactionCard transaction={mockTransaction} />);
    expect(screen.getByText('Grocery shopping')).toBeOnTheScreen();
    expect(screen.getByText('-$45.99')).toBeOnTheScreen();
  });

  it('calls onPress when tapped', async () => {
    const onPress = jest.fn();
    await renderWithProviders(<TransactionCard transaction={mockTransaction} onPress={onPress} />);
    await fireEvent.press(screen.getByText('Grocery shopping'));
    expect(onPress).toHaveBeenCalledWith('1');
  });
});
```

